---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/href';
import { week1 as w1, week2 as w2, week3 as w3, week4 as w4 } from '../../lib/interactives';

const withHref = (items) => items.map((i) => ({ ...i, href: href(i.href) }));
const week1 = withHref(w1), week2 = withHref(w2), week3 = withHref(w3), week4 = withHref(w4);
---

<BaseLayout title="Interactives — ECE/ME 732">
  <h1>Interactives</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Tools for building intuition, grouped by the week they support. Everything runs in
    the browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Week 4: The DC motor, and modifying its feedback</h2>
  <ul class="card-list">
    {week4.map((item) => (
      <li>
        <a href={item.href}><h2>{item.title}</h2></a>
        <p class="lecture-ref">{item.lecture}</p>
        <p>{item.blurb}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 3: Disturbance rejection and dynamic stiffness</h2>
  <ul class="card-list">
    {week3.map((item) => (
      <li>
        <a href={item.href}><h2>{item.title}</h2></a>
        <p class="lecture-ref">{item.lecture}</p>
        <p>{item.blurb}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 2: State block diagrams, physical feedback, nonlinear models</h2>
  <ul class="card-list">
    {week2.map((item) => (
      <li>
        <a href={item.href}><h2>{item.title}</h2></a>
        <p class="lecture-ref">{item.lecture}</p>
        <p>{item.blurb}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 1: Laplace, second-order response, eigenvectors</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    The numerics are in <code>src/lib/linalg.ts</code> and are checked
    against known closed-form results.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-list">
    {week1.map((item) => (
      <li>
        <a href={item.href}><h2>{item.title}</h2></a>
        <p class="lecture-ref">{item.lecture}</p>
        <p>{item.blurb}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h3>Why these three</h3>
  <p>
    Week 1 sets up one identity the rest of the course leans on: the poles of a
    transfer function are the eigenvalues of the state-space <code>A</code> matrix,
    and the eigenvectors say what shape of motion each one belongs to. The three
    tools approach that from the two directions the lecture uses — geometric
    (what does the matrix <em>do</em> to a vector) and modal (what independent
    motions is a coupled system made of) — and then join them on the s-plane.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
